n. I'm also noting a symptom example like \"fever\" that isn't actually a disease name.\n\nI can confirm \"fever\" is an outlier since other symptom terms like ichthyosis and spasticity go unannotated in that doc, so it's likely an annotation inconsistency rather than part of the real pattern. The true distinguishing feature is syntactic: true positives have \"deficiency\" as the head of a compact noun compound (G6PD deficiency) while the errors use the prepositional form \"deficiency in/of X,\" which the model fails to recognize as denoting the disease itself rather than a biochemical state plus a separate protein entity. I'll frame the pattern insight around this enzyme-deficiency prepositional construction, note the span should extend across the full PP when the context signals a heritable/metabolic disorder, and flag the fever case as noise to exclude from the rule.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The three core discrepancies (Examples 1–3) share a construction that the True Positives lack. Each gold span is a *deficiency-type head + \"of\"/\"in\" + enzyme or gene-product name*: \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase,\" \"deficiency in G6PD,\" \"deficiency of hepatic phenylalanine hydroxylase.\" The True Positives name diseases with a nominal head (*Coats disease*, *ovarian cancer*, *Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome*) or an acronym (*SLS*). Document 1303173 is the key control: its gold-labeled \"G6PD deficiency\" (772–787) is a noun-noun compound with no preposition, and the model handles it.\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The model appears to treat \"deficiency/deficient activity\" as a biochemical state and the enzyme name as a protein/gene entity (outside the schema), so it assigns O to the whole span. The prepositional complement pushes the span boundary past the disease head, and the enzyme name is the component that makes the span look non-disease.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** The trigger explains Examples 1–3. It does not explain Example 4 (\"fever\"). \"Fever\" is a presenting sign in a coordinated list (\"fever, arthritis, and cutaneous vasculitis\") and has no deficiency construction. Its gold label is also inconsistent with the controls: in 10577908, symptom-like terms such as *ichthyosis* and *spasticity* are unannotated, so \"fever\" as SpecificDisease looks like annotation noise or a guideline edge case rather than part of this cluster.\n\n**Step 2: Formulate Pattern**\n\n- **Pattern Insight: Enzyme-Deficiency Prepositional Disease Mentions**\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** A deficiency-type head noun (*deficiency*, *deficient activity*) followed by a prepositional complement (*of*/*in*) naming an enzyme or gene product (FALDH, PAH, G6PD). The model reads the head as a generic biochemical state and the complement as a gene/protein, so it outputs O.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** True Positives name diseases as fixed nominal compounds (\"G6PD deficiency,\" \"Coats disease,\" \"ovarian cancer\") or acronyms (\"SLS\"), with no post-head prepositional phrase. The disease head is the final noun, so the model recognizes the span as a condition name.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label the full span as SpecificDisease when all of the following hold:\n    1. The head is *deficiency* or *deficient activity* (or *deficiency* heading a compound).\n    2. It takes a complement via *of* or *in* that names an enzyme, protein, or gene (e.g., FALDH, PAH, G6PD).\n    3. The context signals a heritable or inborn condition (e.g., \"autosomal recessive,\" \"caused by,\" \"mutations,\" \"syndrome,\" \"characterized by\").\n\n    Extend the span to include the complement and treat the enzyme name as part of the disease mention, not as a separate gene entity. Exclude symptom-only mentions such as \"fever\" from this rule, and check them against guideline consistency separately.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_detail